1
00:00:00,319 --> 00:00:05,320
Google created blueprints and safe rollout practices so that you can

2
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:09,539
follow the best practices and avoid the general mistakes.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,340
A Google Cloud blueprint is a pre‑built production‑ready

4
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:17,780
Infrastructure as a Code template created by Google engineers.

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:22,159
It includes best practices, security standards by default,

6
00:00:22,479 --> 00:00:27,309
IAM patterns, logging and monitoring, and safe rollout configurations.

7
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:30,569
Blueprints are shipped through Cloud Foundation Toolkit,

8
00:00:30,780 --> 00:00:33,380
which, in short, is also called a CFT.

9
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:36,849
These modules are used by Fortune 500 companies,

10
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:40,200
governments, startups, and financial institutions.

11
00:00:40,619 --> 00:00:43,220
Blueprints solve the three biggest problems.

12
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:45,979
First one is inconsistent configuration.

13
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:49,959
Engineers writing the infrastructure code manually make

14
00:00:49,959 --> 00:00:52,450
subtle mistakes like wrong subnet,

15
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,840
wrong VPC. Blueprints eliminate those mistakes.

16
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,240
Another problem it solves is reinventing infrastructure.

17
00:00:59,569 --> 00:01:03,959
Every company should not design their own VPC, firewall, etc.

18
00:01:04,169 --> 00:01:06,919
Google knows the best recommended patterns,

19
00:01:07,239 --> 00:01:10,019
and blueprint helps you to encode that.

20
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:12,800
Another problem it solves is in unsafe deployments.

21
00:01:12,919 --> 00:01:15,510
Blueprints often include the built‑in strategies,

22
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:22,099
so you inherently deploy safer architectures, even when you use IEAC.

23
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,639
How you roll out the changes matters.

24
00:01:25,129 --> 00:01:27,080
Google recommends several techniques.

25
00:01:27,330 --> 00:01:30,309
The first one is a blue‑green infrastructure change.

26
00:01:30,610 --> 00:01:33,889
It means that you build the new infrastructure in parallel,

27
00:01:33,970 --> 00:01:35,199
and once everything works,

28
00:01:35,410 --> 00:01:40,489
you switch traffic over and delete the older version only after the validation.

29
00:01:40,839 --> 00:01:42,760
Another way is a canary rollout.

30
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:43,440
In this,

31
00:01:43,599 --> 00:01:48,510
you deploy the changes to a small portion of the environment first, for example,

32
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:52,480
deploy a new version of a service to just 5% of the parts,

33
00:01:52,599 --> 00:01:58,199
validate everything looks good, then you deploy the remaining 95%.

34
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:00,949
Another way is gradual IAM changes.

35
00:02:01,099 --> 00:02:03,819
IAM is one of the most dangerous areas to modify.

36
00:02:04,029 --> 00:02:06,300
Google recommends introducing new roles first,

37
00:02:06,419 --> 00:02:07,690
test with the small groups,

38
00:02:07,699 --> 00:02:11,080
and removing all permissions only after the validation.

39
00:02:11,259 --> 00:02:15,940
Another one is a node pool rotation instead of in‑place upgrades.

40
00:02:16,169 --> 00:02:21,565
Rotation creates new nodes, drains workload, and deletes old nodes safely.

41
00:02:21,875 --> 00:02:25,714
Lastly, using feature flag for risky infra changes.

42
00:02:25,804 --> 00:02:28,725
This is common in Terraform or GitHub's pipeline.

43
00:02:28,835 --> 00:02:32,395
Instead of immediately deploying a risky infra changes,

44
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:35,074
teams wrap it inside a feature flag.

45
00:02:35,195 --> 00:02:38,214
This lets you toggle features progressively.
